======================================================================
ATTACK 5: TRANSPOSE PAIRING STRUCTURE
======================================================================


Every tournament T has a transpose T^op (reverse all arcs).
H(T) = H(T^op) always. If T ≅ T^op, T is self-dual.

For non-self-dual tournaments, T and T^op form a PAIR.
These pairs always share the same I(x).
Could the collision groups simply be {T, T^op} pairs?

  Collision analysis via transpose pairing:
    classes [11, 15]: transposes = [15, 11], TRANSPOSE PAIR
    classes [17, 20]: transposes = [20, 17], TRANSPOSE PAIR
    classes [28, 36]: transposes = [36, 28], TRANSPOSE PAIR
    classes [32, 35]: transposes = [35, 32], TRANSPOSE PAIR
    classes [42, 45]: transposes = [45, 42], TRANSPOSE PAIR
    classes [51, 52]: transposes = [52, 51], TRANSPOSE PAIR

======================================================================
ATTACK 6: SKEW-ADJACENCY MATRIX SPECTRUM
======================================================================


The skew-adjacency matrix S[i][j] = A[i][j] - A[j][i] is ANTISYMMETRIC.
S has purely imaginary eigenvalues (or zero).
The eigenvalues come in ±pairs.
The PFAFFIAN of S is related to H(T).

S captures orientation information that the symmetric adjacency spectrum might miss.

  ATTACK 6 RESULT: 6/13 hard cases resolved by skew spectrum
  With skew spectrum added: 50 distinct / 56 total
  Still 6 collisions


